module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.witness_module_absent_from_the_executed_set

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data witness_module_absent_from_the_executed_set: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "witness_module_absent_from_the_executed_set" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "**a witness module that discriminates correctly is absent from the runner's executed set** (THE CHECK IS RIGHT, ITS ARMS ARE ENROLLED, AND ITS SUBJECT IS REAL -- the only missing thing is membership in the population the required run actually schedules, so every signal a reader consults says covered and none of them is the one that decides.",

    "THE INVALID STATE is a claim module present on disk, correct, and named in no schedule the required lane reads. The harm is that a green verdict is cited as coverage for the class that module owns. A floor verdict names what it EXECUTED; it cannot name what it never saw, and the reader supplies the missing quantifier without noticing.",

    "RUNG FOUND AT: 2 on paper and BELOW THE LADDER in fact. The mechanism exists and would fire; DESIGN.md section 4b(2) makes rung 2 conditional on that mechanism EXECUTING and STAYING ENROLLED, so an unenrolled wall holds no rung at all. This is the inert-lens tier the design document names: the machinery exists and nothing gates on it.",

    "SPECIMEN, gunbc#10688. Three hand-authored consumers still read `item_registry` by bare leaf after the registry was re-keyed on declaration identity, so the whole-graph read missed every time and every row silently took the unexpanded local entry. `test.claim.resolved_rust_fn_service_chain_consumer_witness` CATCHES THIS -- rebuilding with the fix reverted turns its shallow arm red -- and it was green across the whole branch, because the module appears nowhere in `src/v2/workflow/` and therefore never enters the executed set. `verdict=FloorClean` over 3551 claims was silent about it, and a cross-module claim added in the same PR inherited the dormancy: `planned` stayed 3551.",

    "THE DISTINGUISHING FACT, and the reason this is its own row rather than an instance of a narrow-subject check: THE WITNESS AND THE CLASS ARE BOTH PRESENT AND CORRECT. Nothing about the check is wrong. A reader who opens the file finds discriminating arms, a red control and an accurate name; a reader who greps the corpus finds the class covered; a reader who reads the floor verdict finds it clean. The defect is a JOIN that nobody performs -- claim modules on disk against claim modules scheduled -- and it is invisible from either side alone. Compare [[discriminating_arm_built_but_never_enrolled]], where the file itself withholds the arm, and [[a_written_row_is_not_a_firing_mechanism]].",

    "THE CHEAP DETECTOR is a set difference the runner could compute for free: claim modules discovered on disk MINUS claim modules the required lanes schedule, printed with the verdict. Any non-empty remainder is a population the green does not speak for. The floor already prints `outside_this_runs_universe`, so the shape exists; what is missing is that a module absent from every schedule never reaches that accounting at all -- it is outside the universe of the thing that reports being outside the universe.",

    "RECOGNITION RULE: before citing a green run as coverage for a class, ask which SCHEDULE named the claim, not which file declares it. A claim's existence and a claim's execution are two facts, and only the second is what a verdict reports.",

    "FOURTH INDEPENDENT INSTANCE OF ONE SHAPE IN A SINGLE NIGHT, which is the argument that this is a class and not a coincidence: a check whose FORM is right and whose POPULATION is narrower than its name implies. The others, found by three other lanes: regen asking the filesystem a question `seed_retention_frontier` owns; doc_reachability asserting duplicate-key rather than slug-to-file; heal reporting SUCCESS on a head where build was red, because heal does not install that population. Each was individually reasonable and none was written down as a population claim, which is exactly why four of them could stand at once.)",

    "THE SAME ABSENCE HIDES REDS, NOT ONLY FALSE GREENS, and that is a second harm this row did not state. Every instance above is a CORRECT check whose green is cited as coverage; the absence is dangerous because nobody learns the class is unchecked. But a module outside the executed set is also free to BREAK and stay broken: nothing reports it, so the file rots in place while reading as ordinary committed evidence. gunbc#11025, 2026-09-11: three witnesses in `v2.test.long.accumulator_copy_compile_gate` failed at evaluation with `no field 'kind' on type 'NormalizedTree'` -- a real defect, loud when run -- and had been failing for an unknown length of time. The entry is a `LegacyFrozenPathDeferral` in `gunbc.witness_deferral_freeze`, admitted by PATH POLICY with no executing consumer, so all four of its witnesses execute nowhere in any required lane and three of them could sit red indefinitely. The repair was one field; the reason it survived was membership.",

    "SO THE ADMISSION MECHANISM MATTERS, AND IT IS NOT ALWAYS AN OMISSION. The instances above are modules nobody scheduled -- a gap. This one was scheduled OUT, deliberately, by a path-policy rule that admits an entry as deferred: the roster that records it is itself the evidence, and it is honest about what it is. The class is the same because the consequence is identical (no executing consumer), but the DETECTION differs: a gap is found by joining modules-on-disk to modules-scheduled, while a deferral is already written down and needs only to be read as what it says. A deferral roster is therefore a list of modules whose reds nobody will see, and it can be consulted directly rather than derived.",

  ],

  evidence: [],
}
